C.1.0. {"problem": "The request says Delivery should refuse requests that do not say which branch a commit came from, and that only main-line work should be delivered. The change adds a required `branch` string to `request_delivery`, but no precondition, invariant or rule looks at its value. A request from branch 'work' is accepted like any other. Nothing refuses a non-main branch, and the `deploy` call does not pass `branch` to the kernel.", "where": "delivery/delivery.domain.yaml: request_delivery, DeliveryRequest.branch, deploy preconditions and invariants", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.1.1. {"problem": "The handoff sends `target.branch`, which is the commit's authoring branch. In the authoring world, approved commit C1 is on branch 'work' (`main: C1`), because approval merges the commit into main without changing the commit's own `branch` field. Real handoffs will therefore carry 'work', while every delivery fixture and scenario (R1–R6) hard-codes `branch: main`. The fixtures no longer match what Authoring sends. If a main-only check is added later, it would refuse every approved commit.", "where": "authoring/authoring.domain.yaml: handoffs.deliver_approved_commit (branch: target.branch), Commit fixtures C1–C3, versus delivery world fixtures R1–R6", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.1.2. {"problem": "`branch` is a new required parameter on `request_delivery`, which is an interface change. Authoring's handoff targets `interface: 1.x`, and the delivery fixtures use interface 1.0.0 and 1.1.0. Neither domain's `version` is bumped (authoring 16, delivery 6). The composition still pins interface 1 for both members. Callers that do not send `branch` are now refused, and nothing signals this change to existing consumers.", "where": "delivery/delivery.domain.yaml: request_delivery params and version; toprope-self.composition.yaml members", "kind": "other"}
C.1.3. {"problem": "The glossary says 'only commits on main are delivered' (authoring) and that the deploy floor requires 'on main' (delivery). The new `branch` field is not tied to either statement. It is only recorded, never checked or used, so the glossary claims are still not enforced or visible at the declaration level.", "where": "authoring glossary 'main'; delivery glossary 'deploy floor'", "kind": "reference"}
C.2.0. {"problem": "The handoff sends target.branch, which on an authoring Commit is the branch the commit was made on (fixtures C1–C3 are all 'work'; record_commit takes it from the kernel). An approved commit is merged into main, but its own branch field is not changed. Real requests will therefore say 'work', while every updated delivery fixture says 'main'. The release managers' goal of seeing that only main-line work is delivered is not met: the field records the working branch, not main. The fixtures hide this by showing a value the handoff will not produce for these commits.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml handoffs.deliver_approved_commit (branch: target.branch) vs Commit fixtures C1–C3 and delivery.domain.yaml world fixtures R1–R6", "kind": "contradiction"}
